    "eventMetadata": {
     "context_description": "AOC leads the 2028 Democratic presidential nomination market at 20.9 percent, narrowly ahead of Newsom at 17.3 percent and Ossoff at 14.8 percent, reflecting a fragmented field with no dominant candidate two years after the prior cycle. Trader positioning tracks AOC\u2019s elevated profile among progressive voters and recent primary successes, alongside her fundraising and national visibility through candidate endorsements. Newsom\u2019s earlier market share has eased amid questions over California governance and general-election appeal, while Ossoff benefits from Senate reelection momentum in Georgia and perceptions of broader electability. Polls remain fluid, with Buttigieg, Harris, and Shapiro also drawing measurable support in early surveys. The DNC\u2019s finalized primary calendar\u2014South Carolina first, followed by Nevada, New Hampshire, New Mexico, Michigan, and Virginia\u2014adds scheduling uncertainty that could reward candidates strong in diverse early states. Midterm Senate results and any exploratory announcements before 2027 will likely widen or narrow gaps among these contenders.",
